Link libpython into the C++ ctest executables.

On macOS, conda-forge's Python reports a static LDLIBRARY (libpythonX.Y.a)
via sysconfig, so OpenUSD forces PXR_PY_UNDEFINED_DYNAMIC_LOOKUP ON. In
that mode the USD C++ libraries reference Python C-API symbols (TfPython,
boost::python glue) that are left undefined for resolution at runtime, and
libpython is only meant to be linked into executables. Installed binaries
get libpython through the generator expression added to the boost "python"
target's interface (see link_python_to_executables.patch), but that hookup
does not take effect for the executables built by pxr_build_test, so they
abort at load with errors such as:

    dyld: symbol not found in flat namespace '__Py_NoneStruct'

(also _PyClassMethod_Type, _PyExc_ValueError). This regressed the whole
C++ ctest suite on macOS going from v26.05 (949/949 passing) to v26.08
(195/991 failing).

Link libpython (Python3::Python) directly and unconditionally into the
test executables when PXR_PY_UNDEFINED_DYNAMIC_LOOKUP is enabled, rather
than relying on the interface generator expression. These test
executables are never installed, so only the test binaries are affected;
the shipped libraries are unchanged.

diff --git a/cmake/macros/Public.cmake b/cmake/macros/Public.cmake
index 5f6a545..ee0dbb6 100644
--- a/cmake/macros/Public.cmake
+++ b/cmake/macros/Public.cmake
@@ -663,6 +663,18 @@ function(pxr_build_test TEST_NAME)
         ${bt_LIBRARIES}
     )

+    # When PXR_PY_UNDEFINED_DYNAMIC_LOOKUP is enabled (forced ON on
+    # conda-forge macOS, where sysconfig reports a static Python LDLIBRARY),
+    # the USD libraries leave Python C-API symbols undefined for resolution at
+    # runtime and only executables are meant to link libpython. The generator
+    # expression that wires this up for the installed binaries does not take
+    # effect for these test executables, so they abort at load with errors
+    # such as "symbol not found in flat namespace '__Py_NoneStruct'". Link
+    # libpython explicitly here; these test executables are never installed.
+    if(PXR_PY_UNDEFINED_DYNAMIC_LOOKUP)
+        target_link_libraries(${TEST_NAME} PUBLIC Python3::Python)
+    endif()
+
     # Find libraries under the install prefix, which has the core USD
     # libraries.
     _pxr_init_rpath(rpath "tests")
